WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 established Orontes's structure: a jar at $12.00, variable costs of $7.20, a contribution margin of $4.80, fixed costs of $12,624,000, a breakeven of 2,630,000 jars and actual sales of 3,500,000. Book 1 recorded an effective tax rate of 25.0 per cent. Every figure in this chapter is built from those six.
2.1  Income at any volume
Three routes reach the same answer, and the exam uses all three.
The contribution route is the quickest. Multiply the volume by the contribution margin per unit and deduct the fixed costs. At 3,000,000 jars: 3,000,000 × 4.80 = 14,400,000 of contribution, and 14,400,000 − 12,624,000 = 1,776,000 of operating income. At 4,000,000 jars: 4,000,000 × 4.80 = 19,200,000, and 19,200,000 − 12,624,000 = 6,576,000.
The equation route writes the income statement as a sentence: operating income is price times units, less variable cost times units, less fixed costs. It is slower and it is the one to use when a question changes two things at once, because every element is visible.
The incremental route is the fastest of all and the one a manager wants. Since fixed costs do not move, the change in operating income is simply the change in units times the contribution margin. Moving from 3,500,000 to 4,000,000 jars adds 500,000 jars, which adds 500,000 × 4.80 = 2,400,000 — and indeed 6,576,000 − 4,176,000 = 2,400,000.
That third route is the whole of decision analysis in one sentence, and Chapter 5 generalises it: what matters is what changes.
Figure F702-02 is the schedule.
Figure F702-02. Orontes at six volumes
@table
Jars sold | Contribution at 4.80 | Fixed costs | Operating income
2,000,000 | 9,600,000 | 12,624,000 | (3,024,000)
2,630,000 | 12,624,000 | 12,624,000 | Nil
3,000,000 | 14,400,000 | 12,624,000 | 1,776,000
3,500,000 | 16,800,000 | 12,624,000 | 4,176,000
4,000,000 | 19,200,000 | 12,624,000 | 6,576,000
4,500,000 | 21,600,000 | 12,624,000 | 8,976,000
@end
Read down the last column and the leverage is visible without any ratio: a 14 per cent rise in volume from 3,500,000 to 4,000,000 jars raises operating income by 57 per cent, and a 14 per cent fall to 3,000,000 takes it down by the same proportion. Below 2,630,000 the company loses money, and at 2,000,000 jars it loses $3,024,000 — because the fixed costs are still $12,624,000 whatever the factory does.
