2.2  The two charts
The relationships can be drawn, and the exam asks what each line means rather than for a drawing.
The cost-volume-profit chart puts volume on the horizontal axis and dollars on the vertical. The total revenue line starts at the origin and rises at the selling price. The total cost line starts at the level of the fixed costs and rises at the variable cost per unit. The two cross at the breakeven point. The vertical distance between them to the right of that crossing is operating income; to the left it is loss.
The profit-volume chart is simpler and more useful. It plots only operating income against volume: a single line starting at minus the fixed costs when volume is nil, crossing zero at the breakeven, and rising at the contribution margin per unit thereafter. For Orontes that line starts at minus $12,624,000, crosses zero at 2,630,000 jars and rises $4.80 for every jar after that.
Three readings come straight off the profit-volume line, and all three are examinable.
The slope is the contribution margin per unit. A steeper line means each unit does more work, which comes from a higher price or lower variable costs.
The intercept is the fixed costs, with the sign reversed. A line starting lower means a heavier fixed base and a company that needs more volume before it earns anything.
The horizontal intercept is the breakeven. Anything that raises the slope or lifts the intercept moves it to the left, which is the direction a company wants.
Figure F702-03 sets out what moves the line.
Figure F702-03. What each change does to the profit-volume line
@table
Change | Slope | Starting point | Breakeven
Selling price rises | Steeper | Unchanged | Moves left
Variable cost per unit rises | Flatter | Unchanged | Moves right
Fixed costs rise | Unchanged | Lower | Moves right
Fixed costs replace variable costs | Steeper | Lower | Moves right, and profits rise faster beyond it
@end
That last row is the one worth dwelling on, because it is the structural choice every manufacturer faces. Automating a process converts variable labour into fixed depreciation: the contribution margin per unit rises, so the line is steeper, and the fixed base is heavier, so the line starts lower. The company earns more at high volumes and loses more at low ones — which is exactly the degree of operating leverage that Book 1 measured for Orontes at 4.02, and the reason a food manufacturer with a falling margin should think twice before automating further.
