2.3  Income taxes
Everything so far has been computed before tax, and for most decisions that is correct: tax follows the profit rather than driving it. But a target stated by a board is usually stated after tax, and the exam exploits the difference.
The rule is one line. Tax is charged on operating income less interest, so a target net income must be converted to a target pre-tax income before it can be added to the fixed costs. Divide the after-tax target by one less the tax rate.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: a target stated after tax
Step 1. The target. Orontes's board wants net income of $4,500,000. Its effective tax rate is 25 per cent.
Step 2. Convert it. The pre-tax income needed is 4,500,000 ÷ 0.75 = 6,000,000.
Step 3. Add the fixed costs. 12,624,000 + 6,000,000 = 18,624,000 of contribution is required.
Step 4. Find the volume. 18,624,000 ÷ 4.80 = 3,880,000 jars, which is 3,880,000 − 3,500,000 = 380,000 jars more than the company sold.
Check. The conversion is the whole question. A candidate who adds $4,500,000 to the fixed costs computes 17,124,000 ÷ 4.80 = 3,567,500 jars and understates the requirement by more than three hundred thousand jars — because at that volume the company earns $4,500,000 before tax and only $3,375,000 after it. Divide by one less the tax rate, every time.
Two further points complete the outcome.
Tax does not move the breakeven point. At breakeven, operating income is nil, so the tax on it is nil, and a company that loses money pays no tax on the loss. The whole of the tax adjustment lives above the breakeven, which is why Chapter 1's formula needed no tax term.
Tax scales every answer above the breakeven by the same factor. Operating income of $4,176,000 at a 25 per cent rate is 4,176,000 × 0.75 = 3,132,000 of net income, and every figure in the Figure F702-02 schedule can be converted the same way. That also means a change in the tax rate changes none of the operating decisions in this book: a project that is better before tax is better after it, unless the two alternatives are taxed differently, which is the case Chapter 9 examines.
Figure F702-04 sets the conversions out.
Figure F702-04. Before tax and after, at a 25 per cent rate
@table
Jars sold | Operating income | Tax at 25% | Net income
2,630,000 | Nil | Nil | Nil
3,000,000 | 1,776,000 | 444,000 | 1,332,000
3,500,000 | 4,176,000 | 1,044,000 | 3,132,000
3,880,000 | 6,000,000 | 1,500,000 | 4,500,000
4,000,000 | 6,576,000 | 1,644,000 | 4,932,000
@end
EXAM TRAP   The tax conversion runs one way
To go from an after-tax target to a pre-tax requirement, divide by one less the rate. To go from a pre-tax figure to an after-tax one, multiply by one less the rate. Reversing the two is the commonest error on this outcome.
Interest is deducted before tax and after operating income, so a question that gives interest expense wants it added to the fixed costs — or, more precisely, treated as a further fixed deduction — before the tax conversion.
A loss generates no tax at the simple rate assumed here. Where a question allows a loss to be carried back or forward, say so and compute it.
