2.4  The four levers
Four things can be changed, and a question that asks a company to improve its position is asking which of them to pull.
Raise the price. A five per cent rise takes the jar to $12.60 and the contribution margin to 12.60 − 7.20 = 5.40. At the same volume, operating income becomes 3,500,000 × 5.40 = 18,900,000 of contribution less fixed costs, which is 18,900,000 − 12,624,000 = 6,276,000 — a rise of half as much again, from a five per cent price move. The breakeven falls to 12,624,000 ÷ 5.40 = 2,337,778 jars. The risk is the volume response, which Chapter 11 measures as elasticity.
Cut the variable cost. A five per cent saving takes the variable cost to $6.84 and the margin to 12.00 − 6.84 = 5.16, which at the same volume gives 3,500,000 × 5.16 = 18,060,000 of contribution and 18,060,000 − 12,624,000 = 5,436,000 of operating income. Less powerful than the price rise, because the variable cost is a smaller base than the price.
Cut the fixed costs. A saving of $1,000,000 adds exactly $1,000,000 to operating income at any volume and moves the breakeven to 11,624,000 ÷ 4.80 = 2,421,667 jars. It is the only lever whose effect does not depend on volume.
Sell more. Each extra jar adds $4.80, so an extra 500,000 jars adds 500,000 × 4.80 = 2,400,000 — as section 2.1 showed.
Figure F702-05 ranks them on Orontes's own figures.
Figure F702-05. Four five per cent moves, compared
@table
Lever | The change | Operating income | Breakeven in jars
As reported | | 4,176,000 | 2,630,000
Price up 5 per cent | Jar at 12.60 | 6,276,000 | 2,337,778
Variable cost down 5 per cent | Variable cost at 6.84 | 5,436,000 | 2,446,512
Fixed costs down 5 per cent | Fixed at 11,992,800 | 4,807,200 | 2,498,500
Volume up 5 per cent | 3,675,000 jars | 5,016,000 | 2,630,000
@end
The ranking is the lesson, and it is the same in almost every business: the price is the most powerful lever, the variable cost the second, volume the third and the fixed cost the fourth. That is the arithmetic behind Book 1's finding that two points of gross margin were worth $840,000 a year, and behind the warning that a company buying volume with price is pulling the strongest lever in the wrong direction.
