LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Income"
English income means several different things in one paragraph of this chapter. Operating income is profit before interest and tax; net income is profit after both; target net income is a goal; and income taxes are the charge on it. Outside accounting, income means what a person receives, which is closer to revenue than to profit — and that everyday sense is the one candidates import when they read target income as a sales target. Arabic distinguishes الربح, profit, from الدخل, income, and uses الربح for all the measures in this chapter. French uses résultat for every profit measure — résultat d'exploitation, résultat net — and reserves revenu for what a person receives, which makes the French reading of this chapter the least ambiguous of the three.
