Chapter summary
Figure F702-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F702-07. Chapter 2 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Operating income at any volume is units times the contribution margin less the fixed costs, so Orontes earns 3,000,000 × 4.80 = 14,400,000 less 12,624,000, which is 1,776,000 at three million jars and 6,576,000 at four million — and the incremental route is quicker still, since the change is simply the change in units times $4.80. Drawn, the profit-volume line starts at minus the fixed costs, crosses zero at the breakeven and rises at the contribution margin, so a steeper line comes from a better margin and a lower start from a heavier fixed base; automation does both at once, which is operating leverage. Tax does not move the breakeven, because no tax is paid on nil income, and it scales everything above it: a net target of $4,500,000 at 25 per cent needs 4,500,000 ÷ 0.75 = 6,000,000 before tax, so 12,624,000 + 6,000,000 = 18,624,000 of contribution and 18,624,000 ÷ 4.80 = 3,880,000 jars. Four levers move the answer, and on Orontes's figures a five per cent price rise is worth more than a five per cent cost cut, which is worth more than a five per cent volume gain. And every row of the schedule holds only inside the relevant range, so the middle of the table can be relied on and its ends cannot.
