Case-style practice: three proposals for next year
Orontes's management committee has three proposals for 2026. The sales director wants to cut the price by 40 cents to win volume, and expects 8 per cent more jars. The operations director wants to spend $1,500,000 on automation that would cut the variable cost by 50 cents a jar and raise fixed costs by that amount each year. The marketing manager wants a $600,000 campaign and expects 5 per cent more volume. The jar sells at $12.00 with variable costs of $7.20, fixed costs are $12,624,000, volume is 3,500,000 jars, and the tax rate is 25 per cent.
Required. Compute the operating income under each proposal and rank them. Say which you would recommend and what would change your answer. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC2-1  Answer A. Contribution first, then the fixed costs.
B is wrong. The price has not covered the variable cost of its own unit.
C is wrong. That is the breakeven formula.
D is wrong. That is gross profit, which contains fixed factory costs.
SC2-2  Answer A. 14,400,000 − 12,624,000 = 1,776,000.
B is wrong. That is the contribution, before fixed costs.
C is wrong. That is the income at 3,500,000 jars.
D is wrong. The figure is a profit, not a loss.
SC2-3  Answer A. 500,000 × 4.80 = 2,400,000.
B is wrong. That multiplies by the price, ignoring variable costs.
C is wrong. Unchanged fixed costs are why the whole contribution falls through.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
SC2-4  Answer A. Each unit adds its contribution margin.
B is wrong. The price is the slope of the revenue line on the other chart.
C is wrong. Fixed costs set the starting point.
D is wrong. The breakeven is where the line crosses zero.
SC2-5  Answer A. With no units sold, the loss is the fixed costs.
B is wrong. Zero is the breakeven, not the start.
C is wrong. At zero volume there are no variable costs.
D is wrong. The breakeven is to the right of the start.
SC2-6  Answer A. Higher margin, heavier fixed base.
B is wrong. Removing variable cost raises the margin.
C is wrong. A heavier fixed base raises the breakeven.
D is wrong. Both slope and intercept move.
SC2-7  Answer A. Divide by one less the rate.
B is wrong. That converts a pre-tax figure into an after-tax one.
C is wrong. Rates are not added to amounts.
D is wrong. The tax is not yet known.
SC2-8  Answer A. 4,500,000 ÷ 0.75 = 6,000,000.
B is wrong. That applies the rate the wrong way.
C is wrong. This deducts the tax from the target.
D is wrong. That is the after-tax figure.
SC2-9  Answer A. No income means no tax.
B is wrong. The breakeven is unaffected.
C is wrong. The same objection applies.
D is wrong. The rate scales results above the breakeven only.
SC2-10  Answer A. A price change moves the largest base.
B is wrong. The variable cost is a smaller base than the price.
C is wrong. The fixed cost move is a flat amount.
D is wrong. Volume adds only the contribution margin.
SC2-11  Answer A. A fixed-cost saving is the same at every volume.
B is wrong. The saving does not scale with volume.
C is wrong. It reduces the loss below the breakeven too.
D is wrong. The margin is unaffected by fixed costs.
SC2-12  Answer A. 500,000 ÷ 4.80 = 104,167 units.
B is wrong. That is the cost in dollars, not in units.
C is wrong. This multiplies instead of dividing.
D is wrong. This divides by 12.00.
SC2-13  Answer A. The schedule understates costs at the high end.
B is wrong. The jump is at the top.
C is wrong. The error is real.
D is wrong. It is optimistic, not conservative.
SC2-14  Answer A. Outside the range the assumptions fail.
B is wrong. Tax does not change the contribution margin.
C is wrong. Fixed costs are known within the range.
D is wrong. The price is given.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Contribution margin per unit | 14.00: 40.00 − 26.00 = 14.00.
Operating income at 300,000 units | 1,200,000: 300,000 × 14.00 = 4,200,000, and 4,200,000 − 3,000,000 = 1,200,000.
Operating income at 350,000 units | 1,900,000: 350,000 × 14.00 = 4,900,000, and 4,900,000 − 3,000,000 = 1,900,000.
Breakeven in units | 214,286: 3,000,000 ÷ 14.00 = 214,286.
Units for a 1,500,000 net income target | 357,143: the pre-tax target is 1,500,000 ÷ 0.75 = 2,000,000, so 3,000,000 + 2,000,000 = 5,000,000 of contribution is needed, and 5,000,000 ÷ 14.00 = 357,143.
Operating income with a 5 per cent price rise | 1,800,000: the price becomes 42.00 and the margin 42.00 − 26.00 = 16.00, so 300,000 × 16.00 = 4,800,000 less 3,000,000.
What it tells you | A five per cent price rise adds 600,000 of operating income at unchanged volume, which is half as much again as the company earns, and the same gain would need about 43,000 extra units — a 14 per cent volume rise — to achieve any other way.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P2-01  Answer A. Contribution less fixed costs.
B is wrong. Variable costs have not been deducted.
C is wrong. Contribution is already net of variable costs.
D is wrong. That is gross profit.
P2-02  Answer A. 19,200,000 − 12,624,000 = 6,576,000.
B is wrong. That is contribution before fixed costs.
C is wrong. That is the income at 3,500,000 jars.
D is wrong. That is the increment from 3,500,000 to 4,000,000.
P2-03  Answer A. 500,000 × 4.80 = 2,400,000.
B is wrong. That uses the selling price.
C is wrong. Contribution falls straight through to income.
D is wrong. This halves the correct answer.
P2-04  Answer A. The contribution margin per unit.
B is wrong. The price is the revenue line's slope on the other chart.
C is wrong. The variable cost is inside the margin.
D is wrong. Fixed costs set the intercept.
P2-05  Answer A. The loss at zero volume is the fixed costs.
B is wrong. Zero profit occurs at the breakeven.
C is wrong. At zero volume there are no variable costs.
D is wrong. The breakeven is a volume, not a starting value.
P2-06  Answer A. 1,500,000 ÷ 0.75 = 2,000,000.
B is wrong. That applies the rate the wrong way.
C is wrong. This deducts tax from the target.
D is wrong. That is the after-tax figure.
P2-07  Answer A. No tax is paid at nil income.
B is wrong. The breakeven does not move.
C is wrong. The same objection applies.
D is wrong. The rate scales results above the breakeven.
P2-08  Answer A. The price moves the largest base.
B is wrong. The variable cost is smaller than the price.
C is wrong. A fixed-cost change is a flat amount.
D is wrong. Volume contributes only the margin.
P2-09  Answer A. The saving is the same at every volume.
B is wrong. It does not scale with units.
C is wrong. It reduces losses below the breakeven as well.
D is wrong. The margin is unchanged.
P2-10  Answer A. Those volumes lie outside the relevant range.
B is wrong. The middle is the reliable part.
C is wrong. Step costs are exactly what breaks the ends.
D is wrong. Fixed costs are in every row.
Case-style set: three proposals for next year
The price cut. The jar falls to $11.60, so the margin becomes 11.60 − 7.20 = 4.40, and volume rises 8 per cent to 3,780,000 jars. Contribution is 3,780,000 × 4.40 = 16,632,000, and operating income is 16,632,000 − 12,624,000 = 4,008,000 — below the $4,176,000 the company earns today. The proposal loses money even though it works.
The automation. The variable cost falls to $6.70, so the margin becomes 12.00 − 6.70 = 5.30, and fixed costs rise to 12,624,000 + 1,500,000 = 14,124,000. At the same volume, contribution is 3,500,000 × 5.30 = 18,550,000 and operating income is 18,550,000 − 14,124,000 = 4,426,000 — a gain of $250,000 a year. The breakeven rises to 14,124,000 ÷ 5.30 = 2,664,906 jars.
The campaign. Fixed costs rise to 12,624,000 + 600,000 = 13,224,000 and volume rises 5 per cent to 3,675,000 jars. Contribution is 3,675,000 × 4.80 = 17,640,000 and operating income is 17,640,000 − 13,224,000 = 4,416,000 — a gain of $240,000, almost the same as the automation and without the capital.
The ranking is automation first by a hair, the campaign second, and the price cut last and negative. I would recommend the campaign, because it earns almost the same as the automation, commits no capital, raises the breakeven by far less, and can be stopped after a year if it does not work. Automation raises the fixed base permanently in a company whose margin is already falling, which is the wrong direction for its operating leverage of 4.02.
What would change the answer. If the campaign produced 6 per cent rather than 5, it would beat automation outright; if it produced 3 per cent it would lose money. The whole recommendation rests on a marketing estimate, and the number it must beat is 600,000 ÷ 4.80 = 125,000 jars, or 3.6 per cent of current volume.
